  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a battery design strategy aimed at “all-material utilization,” linking cell architecture to easier recovery and reuse of each component. At the center is a cutaway of a folded pouch-like battery, with labeled internal layers including cathode- and anode-active materials, separator, electrolyte, binder, conductive agent, current collectors, and the outer shell and tabs. The image emphasizes that the battery is built from distinct material classes that can later be separated into “individual materials.”\n\nThe overall message is that thoughtful battery design can support a downstream technology pathway for selective post-treatment rather than bulk recycling. Materials are visually grouped by likely fate: key functional materials are associated with easy repair, metals with easy remanufacture, some non-key materials with easy repurposing, and others with easy degradation. On the left, broad categories such as cathode materials, anode materials, and aluminum metal feed into the battery design, while arrows across the top and bottom suggest a full process from material selection through device design, technological recovery, and post-treatment.\n\nIn short, the figure argues for designing batteries so that nearly every material can be efficiently recovered and directed to its most suitable next use.",
